{% extends "base.html.j2" %}

{% block title %}Trailstory — privacy{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<article class="prose prose-neutral mt-2 max-w-none">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>

  <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed">
    Trailstory is built around a simple promise: your hike is yours. We do not
    keep accounts, we do not run analytics on your photos or your text, and we
    do not retain your raw uploads after the page is rendered.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold">Lifecycle of one upload</h2>

  <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-relaxed">
    <li>
      <strong>Upload received.</strong> Your GPX file and photos are written
      to a temporary directory on the server, identified only by a random
      one-time slug. No name, no email, no account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Parsing &amp; resizing.</strong> We parse the GPX, resize each
      photo to a maximum edge of 1800px, and strip GPS coordinates and other
      identifying EXIF metadata from the resized JPEGs. The originals are
      never re-served.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Narrative call to Anthropic.</strong> A structured prompt — your
      description plus the hike statistics (distance, elevation, duration) —
      is sent to Anthropic's Claude API to write the EN/RU/DE narrative.
      <em>Photo bytes are not sent to the API; only their chronological order
      and a numeric index.</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HTML rendered.</strong> A single self-contained HTML file is
      written, with the privacy-stripped JPEGs embedded as base64 data URIs.
      The file works offline and can be sent over any messenger.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Raw upload deleted.</strong> Your raw GPX and original photos
      are wiped from the temporary directory as soon as the response is
      sent — typically within a second of the page loading. Only the
      privacy-stripped JPEGs and the rendered HTML page remain.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Workspace expiry.</strong> The rendered page stays on disk for
      <strong>{{ retention_minutes }} minutes</strong> so you can re-share
      the link or generate an Instagram carousel. After that window the
      entire workspace is deleted by an automatic sweep.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold">Verify it yourself</h2>

  <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-relaxed">
    Trailstory is open source. Every behaviour described above is enforced
    by the code in this repository — read it, check it, file an issue if
    anything looks wrong:
  </p>

  <ul class="mt-3 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
    <li>
      Raw upload deletion (BackgroundTask):
      <a class="underline font-mono"
         href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/develop/web/storage.py#L205-L215"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">web/storage.py — <code>cleanup_inputs</code></a>
    </li>
    <li>
      Workspace expiry sweep:
      <a class="underline font-mono"
         href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/develop/web/storage.py#L222-L249"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">web/storage.py — <code>sweep_expired</code></a>
    </li>
    <li>
      Per-request workspace + sweep cadence:
      <a class="underline font-mono"
         href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/develop/web/app.py#L137-L149"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">web/app.py — <code>_run_sweeper</code></a>
    </li>
    <li>
      EXIF / GPS stripping during photo resize:
      <a class="underline font-mono"
         href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/develop/trailstory/photos.py"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">trailstory/photos.py</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      Repository root:
      <a class="underline"
         href="{{ repo_url }}"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ repo_url }}</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold">What we do not do</h2>

  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-base leading-relaxed">
    <li>No accounts, no email, no cookies for tracking.</li>
    <li>No analytics on your description, photos, or GPX.</li>
    <li>No third-party scripts other than the Anthropic API call described above
      and Tailwind / Alpine / HTMX assets that load this page.</li>
    <li>No long-term retention. The {{ retention_minutes }}-minute window is the
      maximum, not a minimum.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold">Sent to Anthropic</h2>

  <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-relaxed">
    The prompt sent to the Anthropic API contains: hike statistics derived from
    your GPX (distance, elevation gain, duration), the location name you
    provided (if any), and your description text. Photo bytes are not sent to
    the API; only their chronological order and a numeric index. Anthropic's
    own privacy and retention practices apply to that single API call.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-6">
    <a href="/" class="inline-block rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--rule)] bg-white px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold no-underline">← Back to the builder</a>
  </p>
</article>
{% endblock %}
